WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.240
Hi, I'm Macaron!

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.772
Jev picks from a list but can't write.

00:00:04.772 --> 00:00:07.937
Five repositories each built the same four parts around it.

00:00:09.183 --> 00:00:11.017
A decision model only picks.

00:00:11.017 --> 00:00:15.417
So every harness builds an enumerator, a question format, a confidence gate,

00:00:15.417 --> 00:00:17.251
and a composer for strings.

00:00:19.008 --> 00:00:23.095
The environment's finite action space is compiled into typed questions: pick one,

00:00:23.095 --> 00:00:25.138
yes or no, or a score.

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:28.975
Every question rides one request: one model call per step.

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:34.124
A gate compares the weakest judgment with a threshold set by the action's risk.

00:00:34.124 --> 00:00:37.840
Above it, the action runs, and the trace keeps every probability.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.282
Below it, the step is refused.

00:00:40.282 --> 00:00:44.999
Three refusals end the run with a handoff: the questions, answers and threshold missed.

00:00:45.419 --> 00:00:49.615
The price: the compiler refuses any parameter that would need free text.

00:00:49.615 --> 00:00:52.762
The operator supplies strings by name, before the run.

00:00:54.233 --> 00:00:55.932
System One Harness refuses.

00:00:56.942 --> 00:00:58.486
The search tool drops the result.

00:00:59.651 --> 00:01:02.292
The browser agent escalates to a language model.

00:01:02.712 --> 00:01:05.004
And the diffusion server takes three more reads.

00:01:06.425 --> 00:01:08.876
One probe reads a game from pixels.

00:01:08.876 --> 00:01:12.028
Always answering the commonest key scores point nine seven.

00:01:12.028 --> 00:01:15.179
No encoding beats it; the best scores point five.

00:01:16.883 --> 00:01:20.625
Yet in the project's own demo, Jev plays Mario live: one typed

00:01:20.625 --> 00:01:22.808
decision per step, logged on the right.

00:01:23.228 --> 00:01:26.657
Under the game, the page states where Mario is and what's

00:01:26.657 --> 00:01:27.904
ahead, in plain sentences.

00:01:27.904 --> 00:01:29.463
That adapter makes it work.

00:01:31.142 --> 00:01:33.835
So the model is a policy you can swap.

00:01:33.835 --> 00:01:37.125
The engineering is everything around it, the enumerator most of all.

00:01:39.733 --> 00:01:43.276
To recap: four parts around a model that only picks, and an

00:01:43.276 --> 00:01:45.047
enumerator that makes or breaks it.

00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:47.603
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:47.603 --> 00:01:48.213
I'm Macaron.

00:01:48.213 --> 00:01:48.518
Bye!
